+# Why uhttpd (not busybox httpd): uhttpd serves the docroot index at bare "/"
+# (busybox httpd hard-404s "/"), does proper redirects/error pages, and leaves
+# room for TLS later. It is a documented drop-in replacement for busybox httpd.
+#
+# uhttpd specifics (verified against the pinned source, commit 15346de8):
+#   -p 0.0.0.0:80   HTTP listen
+#   -h /www         docroot (realpath'd; must exist)
+#   -x /cgi-bin     CGI url prefix — any file under <docroot>/cgi-bin is exec'd
+#                   directly as CGI regardless of extension (our binary has none)
+#   -I index.html   index file served for "/" (index.html is a default anyway)
+#   NB: do NOT pass -S. -S sets no_symlinks=1, which makes uhttpd realpath() the
+#       request and enforce docroot-containment — that would resolve our
+#       /www/cgi-bin/portal symlink to /usr/sbin/... (outside /www) and REJECT
+#       it. Without -S the containment check is lexical, so the symlink passes
+#       and the kernel dereferences it at exec. (cgi env: SCRIPT_NAME=
+#       /cgi-bin/portal, PATH_INFO=/<route> — same as busybox httpd, so the CGI
+#       binary is unchanged.)
+#   uhttpd self-daemonizes when -f is absent; it has no pidfile option, so we
+#   track it with pidof and stop it with killall (same as the old httpd script).
+#
+# Listens on 0.0.0.0:80. WAN reachability is governed by nftables (the Open
+# Ports list ships tcp/80), matching the pre-uhttpd behaviour.
 
-[ -x "$DAEMON" ] || exit 0
+DOCROOT=/www
 
